  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "DnD 5.5’s "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Fey Tormentor feat",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/feats/2405532-fey-tormentor"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", introduced in “D&D Beyond Drops” in August of 2026, gives the player options to curse your enemies, both with and without spells. It’s a natural choice for spellcasters."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This feels very similar to feats like Fey Touched and Shadow Touched, but you don’t get to pick the spells, and you get a level 3 spell instead of a level 2 spell. Giving up the freedom of choice may be worth that trade. But because of the AC debuff from Faerie Time Warp and the damage bonus from Hex, characters using this feat are most likely built around spell attacks. Warlocks seem like the obvious choice even though they already get Hex, but others spellcasters eyeing Conjure Minor Elementals (Lore Bard, Sorcerer, Wizard) may enjoy Hex as a low-level, low-cost damage boost."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fey Tormentor requires the "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Fey Pact Origin Feat",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/2024-dnd/feats/fey-pact/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ability-score-increase",
      "heading": "Ability Score Increase",
      "sourceAnchor": "ability-score-increase",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ability-score-increase-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Any mental Ability Score. Perfect for the spellcasters who are likely to take this feat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-faerie-time-warp",
      "heading": "Faerie Time Warp",
      "sourceAnchor": "faerie-time-warp",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-faerie-time-warp-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An excellent debuff. Three targets isn’t a lot, but it’s enough to cover every significant enemy in most combat encounters. -2 to AC is a huge debuff in DnD 5e because AC scales so little, and the speed penalty can stack with the deluge of other speed debuffs to freeze enemies in place. Unfortunately, the debuff only lasts one round and you only get to use it once per Long Rest."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-hags-hex",
      "heading": "Hag’s Hex",
      "sourceAnchor": "hags-hex",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-hags-hex-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Hex is a great spell if you’re leaning into attacks from spells like Scorching Ray. Since this is a General Feat, you’re already high enough level to cast it, and the free casting of Hex means that you could do both in a single turn."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-hags-hex-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bestow Curse is a great debuff, too, but you don’t need to think about the tactics as much."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-5",
      "heading": "What are DnD Beyond Drops?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-5-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Starting in April of 2026, DnD Beyond Drops aim to release monthly content for DnD 5.5 accessible exclusively to users with a paid subscription to DnD Beyond. The included character options are shareable with subscriptions that allow content sharing. For more information, see the official "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "D&D Beyond Drops FAQ",
              "href": "https://dndbeyond-support.wizards.com/hc/en-us/sections/48937182240404"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". The developers also posted "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Reddit AMA discussing Drops",
              "href": "https://www.reddit.com/r/dndbeyond/comments/1t6f0pe/ama_with_ddb_executive_producer_drops_product/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " when they were announced."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-5-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We saw the first round of errata for Drops in "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "August of 2026",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/2222-d-d-beyonds-august-drop-sewers-and-treasures?icid_medium=organic&icid_source=homepage_drops&icid_campaign=2026_drops&icid_content=2026_hype"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", so it appears that WotC plans to continue supporting Drops and to update the published content."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
